{
  "title": "Ilium Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/trauma/1033/ilium-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: May 25 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ilium fractures are high energy pelvic fractures that are often unstable and typically progress from the iliac crest to the greater sciatic notch.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can made with pelvis radiographs but frequently require pelvic CT scan for full characterization.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment may be nonoperative or operative depending on fracture displacement, associated pelvic ring instability and patient activity demands.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Associated injuries",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Iliac wing fractures have **high incidence of associated injuries**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "open injuries",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1033/images/open%20pelvis%201f.jpg",
                      "caption": "Open iliac wing fracture with exposed colon"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "bowel entrapment",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "soft tissue degloving",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pelvic girdle is comprised of",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1033/images/posterior%20pelvis%20with%20ligaments.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows a posterior view of a female pelvis."
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "sacrum",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "2 innominate (coxal) bones",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1033/images/innominate%20bone.jpg",
                      "caption": "Shows the innominate bone."
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "each formed from the union of 3 bones: ilium, ischium, and pubis",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "ilium",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "2 important anterior prominences",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "anterior-superior iliac spine (ASIS)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "origin of **sartorius** and transverse and internal abdominal muscles",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "anterior-inferior iliac spine (AIIS)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "origin of **direct head of rectus femoris** and iliofemoral ligament (Y ligament of Bigelow)",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "posterior prominences",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "posterior-superior iliac spine (PSIS)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "located 4-5 cm lateral to the S2 spinous process",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "3",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "posterior-inferior iliac spine (PIIS)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Plain radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "standard set of **AP pelvis**, **inlet/outlet**, and **judet views**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "helpful for evaluating the iliac wing in addition to pelvic stability and possible acetabular involvement",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "carefully assess CT scan for signs of bowel entrapment",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "evaluate for presence of gas or **air in the soft tissues** which can be associated with open injury or bowel disruption",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "No specific classification for iliac wing fractures",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Generally described as specific subtypes of more common classification systems",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Tile Classification**",
              "table": null,
